---------------------------------

The source code in this repository is published so that it can be read, studied, audited,
copied and referenced. It is NOT open source: copying is permitted, USE is not.

"Open source" (as the term is commonly and formally understood) requires granting everyone
the right to use, modify and redistribute the software. This licence deliberately grants
none of those rights. If you need an open-source licence for this code, you must obtain a
separate written grant from the copyright holder.

WHAT YOU MAY DO
---------------

Everything git and GitHub already let you do with a public repository:

  1. Read, view, study and audit the source code and its history.
  2. Clone, fork and copy the repository, in whole or in part, and keep those copies,
     locally or hosted.
  3. Share verbatim copies, provided this licence and all copyright and attribution
     notices are kept intact and unaltered.
  4. Reference and quote excerpts for the purpose of commentary, review, teaching,
     reporting on, or discussing this project, with attribution to this repository.

WHAT YOU MAY NOT DO
-------------------

Without prior written permission from the copyright holder, you may not:

  1. USE the software, in whole or in part, for any purpose, personal or commercial.
     This includes running or executing it, operating it as or within a service, and
     using it to produce or deliver anything.
  2. Modify the software, or create derivative or adapted works based on it.
  3. Incorporate the software, or any part of it, into another work or product.
  4. Sell, licence, sublicence, rent, lease or otherwise commercially exploit it.
  5. Distribute modified copies, or copies stripped of this licence or of any copyright
     or attribution notice.
  6. Represent the software, or any part of it, as your own work.

POSSESSION IS NOT PERMISSION
----------------------------

Copying is permitted because publishing source through git makes copying inherent: a clone
or a fork IS a copy, and a licence that forbade it would be a rule the medium itself
breaks. Permitting it is a statement of fact, not a grant of anything further.

Being technically able to run a copy you hold is therefore not permission to run it. The
restriction in this licence is on USE, and it survives every copy: the copy is lawful, the
use is not.

